course: dictionary
lesson: 8250
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which meaning to use. «Rhubarb stalks are safe to eat cooked, but rhubarb leaves are poisonous because they contain a lot of oxalic acid.» Here rhubarb is a plant, because the sentence talks about stalks, leaves and eating.
explain: The clues are the words around it: stalks, leaves, eat and poisonous all point to a garden plant. A word can also be a sound or a silly idea, so always check which meaning fits the sentence.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence instead of the hard word. If it still makes sense, you have chosen well. If it sounds wrong, look at the other clues and try another meaning.
fact: Rhubarb is a garden plant with long, thick, pink or red stalks.
fact: «Rhubarb stalks are safe to eat cooked» because they are cooked with sugar in puddings such as rhubarb crumble.
fact: «Rhubarb leaves are poisonous because they contain a lot of oxalic acid.» Never eat rhubarb leaves.
fact: In British theatre, actors mutter "rhubarb, rhubarb" to make the sound of a crowd talking in the background.
fact: In informal British English, rhubarb can also mean nonsense or silly talk.
exercise: «Rhubarb stalks are safe to eat cooked.» What is rhubarb in this sentence? | A type of cloud | A loud argument | *A garden plant whose stalks you can cook | A kind of fish
exercise: «Rhubarb leaves are poisonous.» Which part of the rhubarb plant should you never eat? | The stalks | *The leaves | The sugar | The pudding
exercise: In a play, actors mutter "rhubarb, rhubarb" behind the main characters. What does it sound like? | A garden being watered | *A crowd talking in the background | A baby crying | A loud drum
exercise: «Rhubarb leaves are poisonous because they contain a lot of oxalic acid.» Find and copy the word that tells you why the leaves are dangerous. | *oxalic | safe | poisonous | leaves
test: «Rhubarb leaves are poisonous.» Why are they dangerous? | Because they are cooked with sugar | *Because they contain a lot of oxalic acid | Because they are too sweet | Because they are pink
test: Dad says, "Stop talking rhubarb, I can't follow you." What does rhubarb mean here? | A crowd of actors | *Nonsense or silly talk | A pink garden plant | A pudding for dessert
exam: Which meaning fits «Grandma made rhubarb crumble for pudding»? | *A garden plant cooked as a sweet food | Silly talk | Actors' crowd noise | A dark cloud
